{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CyY7SW4MGez8NiXnFExrAq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — importance_margin_vla_compress\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: **CoRL 2026** (Austin, Nov 9-12; abstract May 26, paper May 29)\n5\t- Timeline: 6 days to abstract, 9 days to paper (as of 2026-05-20)\n6\t\n7\t## Title (working)\n8\t\"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n9\t\n10\t## Core Insight\n11\tVLA token compressibility is determined by the vision connector architecture. Evidence from **5 VLA models spanning 3 connector families** reveals a clean taxonomy:\n12\t- **Linear projection** (π0 3.3B): pruning-immune offline. π0 k=0.1 normalized loss = 0.976 (improves 2.4%). Tokens independently projected, information uniform.\n13\t- **Linear projection + deep post-processing** (X-VLA 880M, linear proj + 12-layer BART encoder): robust k≥0.9 (97.6% SR), **cliff at k=0.7 (56.2% SR, -43pp)**. BART encoder introduces non-linear mixing that creates partial token dependencies. CV=0.151 (between π0=0.038 and OpenVLA=0.215).\n14\t- **Multi-layer MLP** (OpenVLA 7B, OpenVLA-OFT 7B): graded degradation. k=0.1 normalized loss = 1.608 (+61%). Non-linear mixing creates partial dependencies. OFT entropy (0.667) ≈ Standard (0.661), confirming connector — not action head — determines attention structure.\n15\t- **Pixel-shuffle** (SmolVLA 450M): catastrophic cliff-edge. k=0.95 (removing 5%) → 0% SR. Spatial folding creates irreversible entanglement.\n16\t\n17\tKey controlled comparison: π0 (256 tokens, linear) vs OpenVLA (256 tokens, 3-layer MLP) — same token count, same encoder lineage, only connector differs. k=0.1: π0 improves 2.4% vs OpenVLA degrades 61%. This isolates connector as the causal factor.\n18\t\n19\tLearned re-encoding (VTR) overcomes the cliff-edge: cross-attention 60.1±1.3% and MLP adapter 63.4±2.6% at M=32 (vs 0% hard pruning), with M=128 passthrough (58.0%±3.5, 3-seed) confirming the gain comes from re-encoding itself, not compression. SmolVLA M-curve confirmed flat (D105): M=16 (56.9%±1.3) = M=128 (58.0%±3.5), all 5 M-values within ±5pp. VTR achieves near-lossless compression at any ratio for pixel-shuffle connectors.\n20\t\n21\t## Paper Identity (Round 4 Upgrade, 2026-05-20)\n22\t**ANALYSIS + METHOD paper.** (D091 → D097: expanded from 2-3 models to 5 models across 3 connector families)\n23\t\n24\t**Analysis contribution (primary)**: First systematic study of why token pruning catastrophically fails on some VLA architectures. We show that the effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline determines pruning robustness along a continuous spectrum (CV: 0.038→0.314): pure linear projection is immune, linear+deep post-processing shows a cliff pattern, MLP projectors degrade smoothly, and pixel-shuffle collapses catastrophically. 5 models across 4 model sizes (450M-7B) eliminate size confound. No competing work does cross-architecture VLA token analysis.\n25\t\n26\t**Method contribution**: Diagnose-then-Compress framework:\n27\t- Step 1: **Connector Compressibility Diagnostic** (D104: resolved)\n28\t  - **Primary metric: Importance CV** (token importance heterogeneity) — zero-cost, one forward pass\n29\t  - CV forms continuous spectrum predicting pruning tolerance: π0=0.038 < X-VLA=0.151 < OpenVLA=0.215 < SmolVLA=0.314\n30\t  - ⚠️ D106+: X-VLA CV=0.151 correctly predicted its intermediate pruning behavior (k=0.7 ≈50% SR ≈ OpenVLA k=0.7). CV is a valid continuous predictor, NOT a 3-class discrete classifier.\n31\t  - **Key discovery**: X-VLA \"linear projection\" passes through BART encoder (12 transformer layers) before decoder → introduces non-linear mixing → explains CV=0.151 (much higher than π0=0.038 pure linear)\n32\t  - **Entropy as mechanistic supplement**:\n33\t    - π0: entropy=0.959 (per-layer mean, range 0.931–0.983), CV=0.038 — uniform & independent → most tolerant\n34\t    - X-VLA: entropy=0.614, CV=0.151 — selective & BART-mixed → intermediate\n35\t    - OpenVLA: entropy=0.661, CV=0.215 — selective & MLP-mixed → graded\n36\t    - SmolVLA: entropy=0.952, CV=0.314 — near-uniform but spatially entangled → catastrophic\n37\t  - NOTE: X-VLA entropy=0.614 (NOT \"high\"), similar to OpenVLA 0.661. Both go through non-linear mixing.\n38\t  - ✅ Batch 2 complete: continuous CV spectrum confirmed (X-VLA full 8-point curve validates threshold-graded class)\n39\t  - Supplementary: lightweight pruning probe (remove 5%, one forward pass) as practitioner-friendly alternative\n40\t- Step 2: VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding) — lightweight cross-attention module (~22M params) for universal compression\n41\t- For entangled tokens (pixel-shuffle): recovers from 0% to 60%+, M-curve confirmed flat D105 (near-lossless at any compression ratio)\n42\t- For independent tokens (MLP projector): **D109 C4-ext confirmed 0% SR** at 20K steps (M=128, severe overfitting — model outputs identical action every step). VTR on MLP projector does NOT improve SR; reframed as \"VTR ceiling depends on connector type\" finding. User directive: performance degradation acceptable if efficiency improves — VTR value is 75% KV-cache reduction + attention speedup. VTC-only M=32/M=64 training in progress to test whether smaller M values fare better.\n43\t\n44\t**Differentiation from concurrent work**: 4 concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA ICRA'26, See What Matters ICML'26) all propose pruning on MLP-projector VLAs. We (1) explain why pruning fails on pixel-shuffle architectures, (2) identify why pruning succeeds on linear projection (tokens are truly independent), and (3) propose a re-encoding method motivated by the diagnostic analysis.\n45\t\n46\t**Model coverage**: 5 VLA models × 3 connector families × 4 model sizes (450M-7B). RC1 (only 2 models) and RC2 (size confound) fully addressed.\n47\t\n48\t## Core Claims (5 claims, expanded from 4)\n49\t\n50\t### Claim 1: Architecture-Dependent Compressibility (taxonomy — D107 RESOLVED)\n51\tThe effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline determines pruning robustness. Evidence from 5 VLA models:\n52\t- **Pure linear projection** (π0): immune — k=0.1 loss improves 2.4%\n53\t- **Linear + deep post-processing** (X-VLA, BART 12L): **threshold-graded** — immune k≥0.9 (97.6%), threshold at k=0.7 (56.2%, -43pp), then graded degradation k≤0.5 (42.6%→23.0%→~4%→~0%). Distinct from SmolVLA's catastrophic cliff (any pruning → 0%)\n54\t- **Multi-layer MLP** (OpenVLA, OFT): smooth graded degradation. k=0.5 → 37.4% (-49% relative)\n55\t- **Pixel-shuffle** (SmolVLA): **catastrophic cliff** — k=0.95 (5% removal) → 0% SR. Any pruning destroys.\n56\t- **CV predicts degradation onset**: 0.038 (immune) → 0.151 (threshold-graded at k=0.7) → 0.215 (immediate onset) → 0.314 (catastrophic cliff)\n57\t\n58\t**Evidence**: 5 models, 3 connector families, 4 model sizes (450M-7B). Key controlled comparison: π0 (256 tokens, linear) k=0.1 = 0.976 vs OpenVLA (256 tokens, 3-layer MLP) k=0.1 = 1.608. Same token count, same encoder lineage, only connector differs. X-VLA cliff pattern (k=0.9: 97.6% → k=0.7: 56.2%) confirms intermediate CV predicts intermediate behavior.\n59\t\n60\t⚠️ **D108: Pruning method discrepancy** — Offline diagnostic uses L2_norm_zero_out (all models). But LIBERO closed-loop evals differ: OpenVLA = random removal (torch.randperm, physical), X-VLA = L2 norm removal (importance-based, physical). SmolVLA = all methods tested (all 0%). This STRENGTHENS findings (OpenVLA survives even random = hardest test; X-VLA cliffs even with importance-based = easiest test), but paper must accurately report. OpenVLA L2 norm comparison experiment planned for fair cross-model comparison.\n61\t\n62\t### Claim 2: Pixel Shuffle Creates Irreversible Token Entanglement\n63\tSmolVLA's pixel_shuffle connector spatially folds 1024 SigLIP patches into 64 tokens per camera (16x compression). Each resulting token encodes a 32x32 pixel region via channel-to-spatial reorganization. This creates extreme cross-token spatial entanglement: removing any single token eliminates an entire spatial quadrant's information with no redundancy to compensate.\n64\t\n65\t**Evidence**:\n66\t- k=0.95 (remove only 7/128 tokens = 5.5%) = 0% SR — even minimal removal is catastrophic\n67\t- k=0.9 (remove 13/128 tokens) = 0% SR\n68\t- Geometric prior (Sobel edge) k=0.5/0.7/0.9 = all 0%\n69\t- Attention entropy ratio 0.952 (near-uniform, TV=0.05)\n70\t- Official checkpoint Object (59.2% baseline) cliff-edge at k=0.5 → 0%\n71\t- Official checkpoint Spatial (43.8% baseline) cliff-edge at k=0.5 → 0.6% (3/500, CI [0.2%, 1.7%])\n72\t\n73\t### Claim 3a: Linear Projection → Depends on Effective Pipeline (D107: RESOLVED)\n74\tPure linear projection (π0): immune. Linear projection + deep post-processing (X-VLA, BART 12L): \"robust-then-cliff\" — immune at k≥0.9, cliff at k=0.7, then graded degradation. The BART encoder introduces non-linear cross-token mixing that explains intermediate CV=0.151 and the cliff pattern.\n75\t\n76\t**Evidence** (X-VLA LIBERO SR — Batch 1 + Batch 2 COMPLETE, full 8-point curve):\n77\t- k=1.0: **99.2%** (baseline, 496/500) — per-task: [98, 100, 98, 100, 100, 96, 100, 100, 100, 100]\n78\t- k=0.95: **99.0%** (-0.2pp) — per-task: [98, 100, 100, 96, 100, 96, 100, 100, 100, 100]\n79\t- k=0.9: **97.6%** (-1.6pp) — per-task: [96, 98, 100, 92, 100, 96, 100, 98, 96, 100]\n80\t- k=0.7: **56.2%** (-43pp, CLIFF — 281/500) — per-task: [94, 2, 100, 80, 28, 92, 12, 66, 0, 88]\n81\t- k=0.5: **42.6%** (-57pp) — Batch 2 confirmed (MSH fix applied)\n82\t- k=0.3: **23.0%** (-77pp) — graded degradation continues below cliff\n83\t- k=0.2: **~4.0%** (-96pp) — near-zero\n84\t- k=0.1: **~0.0%** (-100pp) — complete failure\n85\t✅ Full curve confirms threshold-graded pattern: immune k≥0.9, cliff at k=0.7, then graded degradation k≤0.5 (42.6%→23.0%→~4%→~0%).\n86\t\n87\tπ0 evidence:\n88\t- π0 (3.3B, 256 tokens, pure linear proj): k=0.1 normalized_L1 = 0.976 (-2.4% = improvement), k=0.5 = 0.983\n89\t- Attention entropy: π0 suffix-to-vision mean 0.959 (near-uniform across 18 layers, range [0.931, 0.983])\n90\t- Importance CV: π0 = 0.038 (lowest in spectrum)\n91\t\n92\t**Key insight**: \"Linear projection\" is NOT sufficient for immunity. The effective pipeline matters. X-VLA's BART encoder (12 transformer layers after linear proj) introduces partial dependencies → CV=0.151 → cliff at k=0.7. π0's pure linear proj → CV=0.038 → immune even at k=0.1.\n93\t\n94\t### Claim 3b: MLP Projector → Graded Degradation\n95\tMulti-layer MLP connectors create partial token dependencies through non-linear mixing, enabling graceful but real degradation.\n96\t\n97\t**Evidence** (OpenVLA 6-point degradation curve):\n98\t- k=1.0: 74.0% (baseline)\n99\t- k=0.95: 69.4% (-6% relative)\n100\t- k=0.9: 62.0% (-16% relative)\n101\t- k=0.7: 49.0% (-34% relative)\n102\t- k=0.5: 37.4% (-49% relative)\n103\t- k=0.3: 20.6% (-72% relative)\n104\t- Entropy ratio 0.639 (progressive sharpening L0=0.98→L29=0.48, TV=0.50)\n105\t- OFT (same connector, different action head): entropy 0.667 ≈ Standard 0.661 → connector determines attention structure, not action head\n106\t- CogACT (MLP projector, literature): 74.8% → 74.2% under 78% pruning (EfficientVLA)\n107\t\n108\t### Claim 4: Learned Re-Encoding Overcomes Cliff-Edge Where Deletion Fails (D072+MLP confirmed → upgraded to METHOD per D091)\n109\tTwo independent re-encoding architectures (~22M params each) validate that **any learned re-encoding** can transform entangled pixel_shuffle tokens into effective representations. **The core mechanism is re-encoding itself, not the specific architecture**: cross-attention (60.1±1.3%, 3-seed) and MLP adapter (63.4±2.6%, 3-seed) both recover from 0% hard pruning, with p≈0.15 confirming no significant difference. M=128 passthrough (~60.0%, 2-seed) achieves +15pp over baseline 44.8%, proving the gain comes from re-encoding rather than compression. M=64 achieves best tradeoff at 62.0±2.3% with 50% compression. Cross-suite generalization confirmed: M=32 recovers to 53.2% on Spatial (vs 0.6% pruning cliff-edge) and 56.4% on Goal. M=96 dip statistically confirmed (p=0.034 vs M=64).\n110\t\n111\t**D109 C4-ext on OpenVLA (MLP projector)**: M=128 VTR at 20K steps = **0% SR** (severe overfitting, model outputs identical action every step). This suggests VTR's effectiveness is connector-dependent: pixel-shuffle benefits enormously (0%→60%+) because re-encoding repairs spatial entanglement, while MLP projector tokens are already individually meaningful and VTR adds unnecessary complexity. VTC-only M=32/M=64 training in progress — if these also fail, VTR is specifically a pixel-shuffle solution, which strengthens the architecture-dependency narrative.\n112\t\n113\t**Evidence** (all confirmed):\n114\t\n115\tCross-attention re-encoding (M sweep, 3-seed where available):\n116\t| M | Compression | SR (3-seed mean±std) | Seeds | Notes |\n117\t|---|------------|-----|-----|-----|\n118\t| 16 | 87.5% | **56.9±1.3%** | 42/43/44 | ✅ ZERO loss vs M=128 |\n119\t| 32 | 75% | **60.1±1.3%** | 42/43/44 | ✅ |\n120\t| 64 | 50% | **62.0±2.3%** | 42/43/44 | ✅ best tradeoff |\n121\t| 96 | 25% | 54.5±3.2% | 42/43/44 | ✅ highest variance |\n122\t| 128 | 0% (passthrough) | **58.0±3.5%** | 42/43/44 | ✅ passthrough control |\n123\t\n124\tMLP adapter control (M=32, ~22.14M params, 3-seed):\n125\t| Architecture | Params | M | Mean SR (3-seed) | Std | Individual seeds |\n126\t|---|---|---|-----|-----|-----|\n127\t| Cross-attention (VTC) | 22.2M | 32 | **60.1%** | ±1.3 | 61.6 / 59.2 / 59.6 |\n128\t| **MLP adapter** | **22.14M** | **32** | **63.4%** | **±2.6** | **65.2 / 64.6 / 60.4** |\n129\t\n130\tTwo-sample t-test: t≈1.94, p≈0.15 → **no significant difference** (α=0.05). Both >> 0% (hard pruning) and > 44.8% (baseline). MLP ≥ VTC in all 3 seeds but difference not statistically significant — confirms re-encoding mechanism, not architecture, is the causal factor. (Note: M=32 seed43 corrected from 62.4% to 59.2% per registry verification; 62.4% was M=96 seed43 CI upper bound misattributed.)\n131\t\n132\t## Story Spine\n133\t\n134\t**Problem**: Vision token efficiency is critical for real-time VLA deployment. Growing literature proposes inference-time token reduction (FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O, ToMe). These methods succeed on some VLAs but their behavior across different architectures is unstudied.\n135\t\n136\t**Gap**: No work has systematically compared token pruning robustness across VLA architectures. The implicit assumption that \"token pruning works or fails uniformly\" is untested. The critical architectural factor determining prunability is unknown.\n137\t\n138\t**Discovery**: We find that the effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline determines token pruning robustness. Testing 5 VLA models (450M-7B):\n139\t- **Pure linear projection** (π0): immune. k=0.1 loss improves 2.4%.\n140\t- **Linear + deep post-processing** (X-VLA, BART 12L): threshold-graded — robust k≥0.9, threshold at k=0.7 (97.6%→56.2%), then graded below.\n141\t- **Multi-layer MLP** (OpenVLA, OFT): smooth graded degradation. k=0.5 → 37.4% (-49%).\n142\t- **Pixel-shuffle** (SmolVLA): catastrophic cliff. k=0.95 → 0% SR.\n143\tCV of token importance (0.038→0.151→0.215→0.314) is a continuous predictor: lower CV = later onset of degradation.\n144\t\n145\t**Solution**: Diagnose-then-Compress framework. Step 1: Token importance CV provides zero-cost diagnostic for pruning tolerance (one forward pass). Step 2: VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding) provides universal compression. For pixel-shuffle: recovers from 0% to 60%+ SR, M-curve confirmed flat (D105: M=16 56.9% ≈ M=128 58.0%, near-lossless at any compression ratio). For MLP projector: D109 C4-ext = 0% SR (overfitting at M=128); VTC-only M=32/M=64 in progress.\n146\t\n147\t**Headline result**: \"Across 5 VLA models spanning 3 connector architectures, we find that pruning tolerance depends on the effective vision-to-LLM pipeline: pure linear projection is immune (π0: loss improves 2.4% at 90% removal), linear projection with deep post-processing shows a cliff pattern (X-VLA: 97.6% at k=0.9, 56.2% at k=0.7), MLP connectors degrade smoothly (OpenVLA: -49% at 50% removal), and pixel-shuffle collapses catastrophically (SmolVLA: 0% at 5% removal). Token importance CV predicts this spectrum. For vulnerable architectures, VTR achieves 60%+ SR at 75% compression where deletion achieves 0%.\"\n148\t\n149\t## Paper Structure (5-model version)\n150\t\n151\t1. **Introduction**: VLA efficiency motivation → diverse connector architectures → 4-class taxonomy discovery → Diagnose-then-Compress framework\n152\t2. **Background**: 5 VLA models, 3 connector families (linear/MLP/pixel-shuffle), token flow analysis\n153\t3. **Experimental Setup**: 5 models, LIBERO benchmark, pruning protocol, VTR compression, diagnostic metrics\n154\t4. **Main Results**:\n155\t   - §4.1: 4-Class Taxonomy Overview (Table 1: 5 models × pruning sensitivity)\n156\t   - §4.2: Catastrophic — pixel-shuffle cliff-edge (SmolVLA, all configs 0%)\n157\t   - §4.3: Graded — MLP degradation (OpenVLA 6-point curve + OFT entropy confirmation)\n158\t   - §4.4: Immune — linear projection (π0 + X-VLA, controlled comparison with OpenVLA)\n159\t   - §4.5: VTR overcomes cliff-edge (M-curve flat + architecture independence)\n160\t5. **Analysis**:\n161\t   - §5.1: Why pixel_shuffle creates entanglement (spatial folding + controlled comparison)\n162\t   - §5.2: Diagnostic metric — Entropy + CV composite (D104, 4-model validation)\n163\t   - §5.3: Re-encoding mechanism (M=128 passthrough + MLP adapter + M-curve flat D105)\n164\t6. **Discussion**: Design implications, limitations (LIBERO only, no real robot), future work\n165\t7. **Conclusion**: 5 claims summary\n166\t\n167\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n168\t\n169\t### RC1: Only 2 models → **FULLY ADDRESSED**\n170\t**Response**: Now 5 models (SmolVLA, OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT, π0, X-VLA) across 3 connector families (pixel-shuffle, 3-layer MLP, linear projection) and 4 model sizes (450M-7B). Each connector class has ≥2 models.\n171\t\n172\t### RC2: Model size confound (450M vs 7B) → **FULLY ADDRESSED**\n173\t**Response**: π0 (3.3B, linear) is pruning-immune while OpenVLA (7B, MLP) degrades 61% at k=0.1. Larger model is MORE vulnerable, not less — directly falsifies \"larger models are more robust\" hypothesis. X-VLA (880M, linear) also immune, matching π0 behavior despite 4× smaller. Size is not the explanatory variable; connector type is.\n174\t\n175\t### RC3: Why not test SmolVLA without pixel_shuffle?\n176\t**Response**: SmolVLA's connector is integral (LLM expects 128 tokens, not 1024). Removing it requires retraining from scratch. OpenVLA comparison isolates the connector's role.\n177\t\n178\t### RC4: Smart compression — is re-encoding or compression the mechanism?\n179\t**Response**: Two independent lines of evidence. (1) M=128 passthrough control: M=128 (no compression) = 59.8% vs M=32 (75% compression) = 61.6%. The +15pp over baseline comes from re-encoding; compression is a free bonus. (2) Architecture control: MLP adapter (65.2%) ≈ cross-attention (61.6%) with CI overlap — re-encoding works regardless of the specific mechanism. Both lines converge: the causal factor is learned re-encoding, not cross-attention specifically nor compression.\n180\t\n181\t### RC5: Relationship to VLA-IAP\n182\t**Response**: VLA-IAP's 97.8% on OpenVLA is consistent with our finding: MLP projector preserves token independence. VLA-IAP untested on pixel_shuffle VLAs; our geometric prior experiments (0%) predict it would fail.\n183\t\n184\t### RC6: Is this just \"different models behave differently\"?\n185\t**Response**: We identify the specific mechanism (pixel_shuffle spatial folding) and provide evidence for WHY. Concrete design implications: future VLAs wanting efficiency should avoid pixel_shuffle or include learned re-encoding modules.\n186\t\n187\t## All Experimental Evidence (confirmed)\n188\t\n189\t### SmolVLA Cliff-Edge\n190\t- Fine-tuned baseline (Object): 44.8% (224/500)\n191\t- 6 pruning conditions (random/ACIS/ToMe × k=0.5/0.7/0.9): all 0%\n192\t- Geometric prior (Sobel) k=0.5/0.7/0.9: all 0%\n193\t- Attention entropy: 0.952 (flat, TV=0.05)\n194\t- Official checkpoint Object: 59.2% → 0% at k=0.5\n195\t- Official checkpoint Spatial: 43.8% → 0.6% (3/500, CI [0.2%, 1.7%]) at k=0.5 — near-zero, confirms cliff-edge across suites\n196\t\n197\t### OpenVLA Degradation Curve\n198\t- k=1.0: 74.0% (370/500)\n199\t- k=0.95: 69.4% (-6% relative)\n200\t- k=0.9: 62.0% (-16% relative)\n201\t- k=0.7: 49.0% (245/500, -34% relative)\n202\t- k=0.5: 37.4% (187/500, -49% relative)\n203\t- k=0.3: 20.6% (103/500, -72% relative)\n204\t- Entropy: 0.639 (progressive sharpening, TV=0.50)\n205\t\n206\t### Cross-Model Pruning Taxonomy (NEW — D100 confirmed all L2_norm_zero_out)\n207\t| Model | Connector | Tokens | k=0.1 norm_L1 | k=0.5 norm_L1 | Class |\n208\t|-------|-----------|--------|---------------|---------------|-------|\n209\t| π0 (3.3B) | Pure linear proj | 256 | 0.976 (-2.4%) | 0.983 | Immune |\n210\t| X-VLA (880M) | Linear + BART 12L | 50 | ~1.00 | ~1.00 | Threshold-graded (k=0.7: 56.2%) |\n211\t| OpenVLA (7B) | 3-layer MLP | 256 | 1.608 (+61%) | 1.167 (+17%) | Smooth graded |\n212\t| OFT (7B) | 3-layer MLP | 256 | pending (M031) | — | (entropy=0.667≈Std) |\n213\t| SmolVLA (450M) | Pixel-shuffle | 128 | — | 0% cliff | Catastrophic |\n214\t\n215\tDiagnostic metrics (D104+D106: CV as continuous predictor):\n216\t| Model | Connector (effective) | Entropy | CV | Pruning Behavior |\n217\t|-------|----------------------|---------|------|------------------|\n218\t| π0 | Pure linear proj | 0.959 | 0.038 | Immune (offline, k=0.1 improves) |\n219\t| X-VLA | Linear proj + BART enc (12L) | **0.614** | **0.151** | Threshold-graded: robust k≥0.9, cliff k=0.7 (56.2%) |\n220\t| OpenVLA | 3-layer MLP (GELU) | 0.661 | 0.215 | Graded (74→37.4% at k=0.5) |\n221\t| OFT | 3-layer MLP (GELU) | 0.667 | — | (entropy≈Standard, confirms connector) |\n222\t| SmolVLA | Pixel-shuffle (16×) | 0.952 | **0.314** | Catastrophic (0% at k=0.95) |\n223\t\n224\tKey insights (updated with X-VLA data):\n225\t1. **CV is a continuous predictor**: 0.038 → 0.151 → 0.215 → 0.314 maps to decreasing pruning tolerance. NOT a 3-class discrete classifier.\n226\t2. **X-VLA CV=0.151 correctly predicted its intermediate behavior** — closer to OpenVLA (0.215) than π0 (0.038), matching k=0.7 SR ≈ OpenVLA k=0.7.\n227\t3. **X-VLA entropy=0.614 ≈ OpenVLA 0.661** — both have BART/MLP non-linear mixing. X-VLA is NOT \"high entropy\" like π0.\n228\t4. **Effective connector complexity matters more than nominal label**: X-VLA is \"linear proj\" but BART encoder introduces 12 transformer layers of mixing. Paper should discuss \"effective connector\" = projection + any intermediate processing.\n229\t5. OFT entropy 0.667 ≈ Standard 0.661 → action head does not affect attention structure\n230\t6. Framing: CV captures \"degree of non-linear mixing in the vision-to-LLM pathway.\" More mixing → higher CV → less tolerant of pruning.\n231\t\n232\t### Smart Compress (Learned Re-encoding)\n233\tCross-attention (VTC) M sweep (3-seed where available):\n234\t- M=16: **56.9% ± 1.3pp** (3-seed: 55.8/58.4/56.5) — 87.5% compression, ZERO loss vs M=128\n235\t- M=32: 60.1±1.3% (3-seed: 61.6/59.2/59.6)\n236\t- M=64: 62.0±2.3% (3-seed: 61.6/60.0/64.4)\n237\t- M=96: 54.5±3.2% (3-seed: 52.6/58.2/52.8) — highest variance\n238\t- M=128: 58.0±3.5pp (3-seed: 59.8/54.0/60.2) — passthrough control\n239\t- **D102 CONFIRMED: M-curve is flat.** M=16 (56.9%) = M=128 (58.0%), difference < 1.1pp. VTC achieves near-lossless compression at ANY ratio for pixel-shuffle connector. All M values within ±5pp of baseline ~59%.\n240\t\n241\tMLP adapter (M=32, 3-seed):\n242\t- seed42: 65.2% (326/500), seed43: 64.6% (323/500), seed44: 60.4% (302/500)\n243\t- Mean: 63.4% ± 2.6 vs VTC M=32 mean 60.1% ± 1.3\n244\t- t-test p≈0.15, no significant difference — confirms architecture-independence\n245\t\n246\t### D109: VTR on OpenVLA (MLP projector) — FAILED\n247\t- C4-ext M=128 at 20K steps: **0% SR** (0/44 episodes before eval termination)\n248\t- Failure mode: model outputs identical action every timestep (severe overfitting)\n249\t- Interpretation: VTR adds unnecessary complexity for MLP projector tokens (already individually meaningful). VTR specifically solves pixel-shuffle entanglement.\n250\t- VTC-only M=32 and M=64 training in progress on westd-41074:2-3\n251\t\n252\t### Cross-Suite VTR Generalization (SmolVLA)\n253\t- M=32 Spatial: 53.2% (vs official Spatial baseline 43.8%, vs pruning 0.6% — recovery from cliff-edge AND exceeds uncompressed baseline)\n254\t- M=32 Goal: 56.4% (vs official Goal baseline 63.0%, vs pruning 0% — recovery from cliff-edge but below uncompressed baseline; frame as compression-recovery, not performance boost)\n255\t- Note: VTR trained on Object suite, evaluated cross-suite. Official baselines from different checkpoint (official vs LoRA-FT); comparisons should emphasize recovery from 0% rather than absolute performance vs uncompressed.\n256\t\n257\t### Pairwise Statistical Tests (Welch's t-test, n=3 per condition)\n258\t- M=32 VTC vs M=32 MLP: p=0.150 (not significant) — confirms architecture-independence\n259\t- M=64 VTC vs M=96 VTC: p=0.034 (significant) — M=96 dip confirmed real\n260\t- M=32 MLP vs M=96 VTC: p=0.022 (significant) — M=96 underperforms\n261\t- M=32/M=64/M=128 VTC cluster: all pairwise p>0.25 — robust across M values\n262\t- Bonferroni note: no pair survives correction at 10 tests; n=3 limits power (~0.2 for medium effects)\n263\t\n264\t### Supplementary\n265\t- Inference latency: 6% speedup at M=32\n266\t- KV-cache: 75% memory reduction at M=32\n267\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ee454c3-2f7e-4079-9b70-bcb44e9beab1","uuid":"35e4422a-73f0-4d8c-b735-c090dd0f1a2e","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:23:21.703Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T155932/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — importance_margin_vla_compress\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: **CoRL 2026** (Austin, Nov 9-12; abstract May 26, paper May 29)\n- Timeline: 6 days to abstract, 9 days to paper (as of 2026-05-20)\n\n## Title (working)\n\"Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others: The Role of Vision Connectors in VLA Token Compressibility\"\n\n## Core Insight\nVLA token compressibility is determined by the vision connector architecture. Evidence from **5 VLA models spanning 3 connector families** reveals a clean taxonomy:\n- **Linear projection** (π0 3.3B): pruning-immune offline. π0 k=0.1 normalized loss = 0.976 (improves 2.4%). Tokens independently projected, information uniform.\n- **Linear projection + deep post-processing** (X-VLA 880M, linear proj + 12-layer BART encoder): robust k≥0.9 (97.6% SR), **cliff at k=0.7 (56.2% SR, -43pp)**. BART encoder introduces non-linear mixing that creates partial token dependencies. CV=0.151 (between π0=0.038 and OpenVLA=0.215).\n- **Multi-layer MLP** (OpenVLA 7B, OpenVLA-OFT 7B): graded degradation. k=0.1 normalized loss = 1.608 (+61%). Non-linear mixing creates partial dependencies. OFT entropy (0.667) ≈ Standard (0.661), confirming connector — not action head — determines attention structure.\n- **Pixel-shuffle** (SmolVLA 450M): catastrophic cliff-edge. k=0.95 (removing 5%) → 0% SR. Spatial folding creates irreversible entanglement.\n\nKey controlled comparison: π0 (256 tokens, linear) vs OpenVLA (256 tokens, 3-layer MLP) — same token count, same encoder lineage, only connector differs. k=0.1: π0 improves 2.4% vs OpenVLA degrades 61%. This isolates connector as the causal factor.\n\nLearned re-encoding (VTR) overcomes the cliff-edge: cross-attention 60.1±1.3% and MLP adapter 63.4±2.6% at M=32 (vs 0% hard pruning), with M=128 passthrough (58.0%±3.5, 3-seed) confirming the gain comes from re-encoding itself, not compression. SmolVLA M-curve confirmed flat (D105): M=16 (56.9%±1.3) = M=128 (58.0%±3.5), all 5 M-values within ±5pp. VTR achieves near-lossless compression at any ratio for pixel-shuffle connectors.\n\n## Paper Identity (Round 4 Upgrade, 2026-05-20)\n**ANALYSIS + METHOD paper.** (D091 → D097: expanded from 2-3 models to 5 models across 3 connector families)\n\n**Analysis contribution (primary)**: First systematic study of why token pruning catastrophically fails on some VLA architectures. We show that the effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline determines pruning robustness along a continuous spectrum (CV: 0.038→0.314): pure linear projection is immune, linear+deep post-processing shows a cliff pattern, MLP projectors degrade smoothly, and pixel-shuffle collapses catastrophically. 5 models across 4 model sizes (450M-7B) eliminate size confound. No competing work does cross-architecture VLA token analysis.\n\n**Method contribution**: Diagnose-then-Compress framework:\n- Step 1: **Connector Compressibility Diagnostic** (D104: resolved)\n  - **Primary metric: Importance CV** (token importance heterogeneity) — zero-cost, one forward pass\n  - CV forms continuous spectrum predicting pruning tolerance: π0=0.038 < X-VLA=0.151 < OpenVLA=0.215 < SmolVLA=0.314\n  - ⚠️ D106+: X-VLA CV=0.151 correctly predicted its intermediate pruning behavior (k=0.7 ≈50% SR ≈ OpenVLA k=0.7). CV is a valid continuous predictor, NOT a 3-class discrete classifier.\n  - **Key discovery**: X-VLA \"linear projection\" passes through BART encoder (12 transformer layers) before decoder → introduces non-linear mixing → explains CV=0.151 (much higher than π0=0.038 pure linear)\n  - **Entropy as mechanistic supplement**:\n    - π0: entropy=0.959 (per-layer mean, range 0.931–0.983), CV=0.038 — uniform & independent → most tolerant\n    - X-VLA: entropy=0.614, CV=0.151 — selective & BART-mixed → intermediate\n    - OpenVLA: entropy=0.661, CV=0.215 — selective & MLP-mixed → graded\n    - SmolVLA: entropy=0.952, CV=0.314 — near-uniform but spatially entangled → catastrophic\n  - NOTE: X-VLA entropy=0.614 (NOT \"high\"), similar to OpenVLA 0.661. Both go through non-linear mixing.\n  - ✅ Batch 2 complete: continuous CV spectrum confirmed (X-VLA full 8-point curve validates threshold-graded class)\n  - Supplementary: lightweight pruning probe (remove 5%, one forward pass) as practitioner-friendly alternative\n- Step 2: VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding) — lightweight cross-attention module (~22M params) for universal compression\n- For entangled tokens (pixel-shuffle): recovers from 0% to 60%+, M-curve confirmed flat D105 (near-lossless at any compression ratio)\n- For independent tokens (MLP projector): **D109 C4-ext confirmed 0% SR** at 20K steps (M=128, severe overfitting — model outputs identical action every step). VTR on MLP projector does NOT improve SR; reframed as \"VTR ceiling depends on connector type\" finding. User directive: performance degradation acceptable if efficiency improves — VTR value is 75% KV-cache reduction + attention speedup. VTC-only M=32/M=64 training in progress to test whether smaller M values fare better.\n\n**Differentiation from concurrent work**: 4 concurrent works (Compressor-VLA, SpecPrune-VLA, LightVLA ICRA'26, See What Matters ICML'26) all propose pruning on MLP-projector VLAs. We (1) explain why pruning fails on pixel-shuffle architectures, (2) identify why pruning succeeds on linear projection (tokens are truly independent), and (3) propose a re-encoding method motivated by the diagnostic analysis.\n\n**Model coverage**: 5 VLA models × 3 connector families × 4 model sizes (450M-7B). RC1 (only 2 models) and RC2 (size confound) fully addressed.\n\n## Core Claims (5 claims, expanded from 4)\n\n### Claim 1: Architecture-Dependent Compressibility (taxonomy — D107 RESOLVED)\nThe effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline determines pruning robustness. Evidence from 5 VLA models:\n- **Pure linear projection** (π0): immune — k=0.1 loss improves 2.4%\n- **Linear + deep post-processing** (X-VLA, BART 12L): **threshold-graded** — immune k≥0.9 (97.6%), threshold at k=0.7 (56.2%, -43pp), then graded degradation k≤0.5 (42.6%→23.0%→~4%→~0%). Distinct from SmolVLA's catastrophic cliff (any pruning → 0%)\n- **Multi-layer MLP** (OpenVLA, OFT): smooth graded degradation. k=0.5 → 37.4% (-49% relative)\n- **Pixel-shuffle** (SmolVLA): **catastrophic cliff** — k=0.95 (5% removal) → 0% SR. Any pruning destroys.\n- **CV predicts degradation onset**: 0.038 (immune) → 0.151 (threshold-graded at k=0.7) → 0.215 (immediate onset) → 0.314 (catastrophic cliff)\n\n**Evidence**: 5 models, 3 connector families, 4 model sizes (450M-7B). Key controlled comparison: π0 (256 tokens, linear) k=0.1 = 0.976 vs OpenVLA (256 tokens, 3-layer MLP) k=0.1 = 1.608. Same token count, same encoder lineage, only connector differs. X-VLA cliff pattern (k=0.9: 97.6% → k=0.7: 56.2%) confirms intermediate CV predicts intermediate behavior.\n\n⚠️ **D108: Pruning method discrepancy** — Offline diagnostic uses L2_norm_zero_out (all models). But LIBERO closed-loop evals differ: OpenVLA = random removal (torch.randperm, physical), X-VLA = L2 norm removal (importance-based, physical). SmolVLA = all methods tested (all 0%). This STRENGTHENS findings (OpenVLA survives even random = hardest test; X-VLA cliffs even with importance-based = easiest test), but paper must accurately report. OpenVLA L2 norm comparison experiment planned for fair cross-model comparison.\n\n### Claim 2: Pixel Shuffle Creates Irreversible Token Entanglement\nSmolVLA's pixel_shuffle connector spatially folds 1024 SigLIP patches into 64 tokens per camera (16x compression). Each resulting token encodes a 32x32 pixel region via channel-to-spatial reorganization. This creates extreme cross-token spatial entanglement: removing any single token eliminates an entire spatial quadrant's information with no redundancy to compensate.\n\n**Evidence**:\n- k=0.95 (remove only 7/128 tokens = 5.5%) = 0% SR — even minimal removal is catastrophic\n- k=0.9 (remove 13/128 tokens) = 0% SR\n- Geometric prior (Sobel edge) k=0.5/0.7/0.9 = all 0%\n- Attention entropy ratio 0.952 (near-uniform, TV=0.05)\n- Official checkpoint Object (59.2% baseline) cliff-edge at k=0.5 → 0%\n- Official checkpoint Spatial (43.8% baseline) cliff-edge at k=0.5 → 0.6% (3/500, CI [0.2%, 1.7%])\n\n### Claim 3a: Linear Projection → Depends on Effective Pipeline (D107: RESOLVED)\nPure linear projection (π0): immune. Linear projection + deep post-processing (X-VLA, BART 12L): \"robust-then-cliff\" — immune at k≥0.9, cliff at k=0.7, then graded degradation. The BART encoder introduces non-linear cross-token mixing that explains intermediate CV=0.151 and the cliff pattern.\n\n**Evidence** (X-VLA LIBERO SR — Batch 1 + Batch 2 COMPLETE, full 8-point curve):\n- k=1.0: **99.2%** (baseline, 496/500) — per-task: [98, 100, 98, 100, 100, 96, 100, 100, 100, 100]\n- k=0.95: **99.0%** (-0.2pp) — per-task: [98, 100, 100, 96, 100, 96, 100, 100, 100, 100]\n- k=0.9: **97.6%** (-1.6pp) — per-task: [96, 98, 100, 92, 100, 96, 100, 98, 96, 100]\n- k=0.7: **56.2%** (-43pp, CLIFF — 281/500) — per-task: [94, 2, 100, 80, 28, 92, 12, 66, 0, 88]\n- k=0.5: **42.6%** (-57pp) — Batch 2 confirmed (MSH fix applied)\n- k=0.3: **23.0%** (-77pp) — graded degradation continues below cliff\n- k=0.2: **~4.0%** (-96pp) — near-zero\n- k=0.1: **~0.0%** (-100pp) — complete failure\n✅ Full curve confirms threshold-graded pattern: immune k≥0.9, cliff at k=0.7, then graded degradation k≤0.5 (42.6%→23.0%→~4%→~0%).\n\nπ0 evidence:\n- π0 (3.3B, 256 tokens, pure linear proj): k=0.1 normalized_L1 = 0.976 (-2.4% = improvement), k=0.5 = 0.983\n- Attention entropy: π0 suffix-to-vision mean 0.959 (near-uniform across 18 layers, range [0.931, 0.983])\n- Importance CV: π0 = 0.038 (lowest in spectrum)\n\n**Key insight**: \"Linear projection\" is NOT sufficient for immunity. The effective pipeline matters. X-VLA's BART encoder (12 transformer layers after linear proj) introduces partial dependencies → CV=0.151 → cliff at k=0.7. π0's pure linear proj → CV=0.038 → immune even at k=0.1.\n\n### Claim 3b: MLP Projector → Graded Degradation\nMulti-layer MLP connectors create partial token dependencies through non-linear mixing, enabling graceful but real degradation.\n\n**Evidence** (OpenVLA 6-point degradation curve):\n- k=1.0: 74.0% (baseline)\n- k=0.95: 69.4% (-6% relative)\n- k=0.9: 62.0% (-16% relative)\n- k=0.7: 49.0% (-34% relative)\n- k=0.5: 37.4% (-49% relative)\n- k=0.3: 20.6% (-72% relative)\n- Entropy ratio 0.639 (progressive sharpening L0=0.98→L29=0.48, TV=0.50)\n- OFT (same connector, different action head): entropy 0.667 ≈ Standard 0.661 → connector determines attention structure, not action head\n- CogACT (MLP projector, literature): 74.8% → 74.2% under 78% pruning (EfficientVLA)\n\n### Claim 4: Learned Re-Encoding Overcomes Cliff-Edge Where Deletion Fails (D072+MLP confirmed → upgraded to METHOD per D091)\nTwo independent re-encoding architectures (~22M params each) validate that **any learned re-encoding** can transform entangled pixel_shuffle tokens into effective representations. **The core mechanism is re-encoding itself, not the specific architecture**: cross-attention (60.1±1.3%, 3-seed) and MLP adapter (63.4±2.6%, 3-seed) both recover from 0% hard pruning, with p≈0.15 confirming no significant difference. M=128 passthrough (~60.0%, 2-seed) achieves +15pp over baseline 44.8%, proving the gain comes from re-encoding rather than compression. M=64 achieves best tradeoff at 62.0±2.3% with 50% compression. Cross-suite generalization confirmed: M=32 recovers to 53.2% on Spatial (vs 0.6% pruning cliff-edge) and 56.4% on Goal. M=96 dip statistically confirmed (p=0.034 vs M=64).\n\n**D109 C4-ext on OpenVLA (MLP projector)**: M=128 VTR at 20K steps = **0% SR** (severe overfitting, model outputs identical action every step). This suggests VTR's effectiveness is connector-dependent: pixel-shuffle benefits enormously (0%→60%+) because re-encoding repairs spatial entanglement, while MLP projector tokens are already individually meaningful and VTR adds unnecessary complexity. VTC-only M=32/M=64 training in progress — if these also fail, VTR is specifically a pixel-shuffle solution, which strengthens the architecture-dependency narrative.\n\n**Evidence** (all confirmed):\n\nCross-attention re-encoding (M sweep, 3-seed where available):\n| M | Compression | SR (3-seed mean±std) | Seeds | Notes |\n|---|------------|-----|-----|-----|\n| 16 | 87.5% | **56.9±1.3%** | 42/43/44 | ✅ ZERO loss vs M=128 |\n| 32 | 75% | **60.1±1.3%** | 42/43/44 | ✅ |\n| 64 | 50% | **62.0±2.3%** | 42/43/44 | ✅ best tradeoff |\n| 96 | 25% | 54.5±3.2% | 42/43/44 | ✅ highest variance |\n| 128 | 0% (passthrough) | **58.0±3.5%** | 42/43/44 | ✅ passthrough control |\n\nMLP adapter control (M=32, ~22.14M params, 3-seed):\n| Architecture | Params | M | Mean SR (3-seed) | Std | Individual seeds |\n|---|---|---|-----|-----|-----|\n| Cross-attention (VTC) | 22.2M | 32 | **60.1%** | ±1.3 | 61.6 / 59.2 / 59.6 |\n| **MLP adapter** | **22.14M** | **32** | **63.4%** | **±2.6** | **65.2 / 64.6 / 60.4** |\n\nTwo-sample t-test: t≈1.94, p≈0.15 → **no significant difference** (α=0.05). Both >> 0% (hard pruning) and > 44.8% (baseline). MLP ≥ VTC in all 3 seeds but difference not statistically significant — confirms re-encoding mechanism, not architecture, is the causal factor. (Note: M=32 seed43 corrected from 62.4% to 59.2% per registry verification; 62.4% was M=96 seed43 CI upper bound misattributed.)\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: Vision token efficiency is critical for real-time VLA deployment. Growing literature proposes inference-time token reduction (FastV, VLA-IAP, H2O, ToMe). These methods succeed on some VLAs but their behavior across different architectures is unstudied.\n\n**Gap**: No work has systematically compared token pruning robustness across VLA architectures. The implicit assumption that \"token pruning works or fails uniformly\" is untested. The critical architectural factor determining prunability is unknown.\n\n**Discovery**: We find that the effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline determines token pruning robustness. Testing 5 VLA models (450M-7B):\n- **Pure linear projection** (π0): immune. k=0.1 loss improves 2.4%.\n- **Linear + deep post-processing** (X-VLA, BART 12L): threshold-graded — robust k≥0.9, threshold at k=0.7 (97.6%→56.2%), then graded below.\n- **Multi-layer MLP** (OpenVLA, OFT): smooth graded degradation. k=0.5 → 37.4% (-49%).\n- **Pixel-shuffle** (SmolVLA): catastrophic cliff. k=0.95 → 0% SR.\nCV of token importance (0.038→0.151→0.215→0.314) is a continuous predictor: lower CV = later onset of degradation.\n\n**Solution**: Diagnose-then-Compress framework. Step 1: Token importance CV provides zero-cost diagnostic for pruning tolerance (one forward pass). Step 2: VTR (Vision Token Re-encoding) provides universal compression. For pixel-shuffle: recovers from 0% to 60%+ SR, M-curve confirmed flat (D105: M=16 56.9% ≈ M=128 58.0%, near-lossless at any compression ratio). For MLP projector: D109 C4-ext = 0% SR (overfitting at M=128); VTC-only M=32/M=64 in progress.\n\n**Headline result**: \"Across 5 VLA models spanning 3 connector architectures, we find that pruning tolerance depends on the effective vision-to-LLM pipeline: pure linear projection is immune (π0: loss improves 2.4% at 90% removal), linear projection with deep post-processing shows a cliff pattern (X-VLA: 97.6% at k=0.9, 56.2% at k=0.7), MLP connectors degrade smoothly (OpenVLA: -49% at 50% removal), and pixel-shuffle collapses catastrophically (SmolVLA: 0% at 5% removal). Token importance CV predicts this spectrum. For vulnerable architectures, VTR achieves 60%+ SR at 75% compression where deletion achieves 0%.\"\n\n## Paper Structure (5-model version)\n\n1. **Introduction**: VLA efficiency motivation → diverse connector architectures → 4-class taxonomy discovery → Diagnose-then-Compress framework\n2. **Background**: 5 VLA models, 3 connector families (linear/MLP/pixel-shuffle), token flow analysis\n3. **Experimental Setup**: 5 models, LIBERO benchmark, pruning protocol, VTR compression, diagnostic metrics\n4. **Main Results**:\n   - §4.1: 4-Class Taxonomy Overview (Table 1: 5 models × pruning sensitivity)\n   - §4.2: Catastrophic — pixel-shuffle cliff-edge (SmolVLA, all configs 0%)\n   - §4.3: Graded — MLP degradation (OpenVLA 6-point curve + OFT entropy confirmation)\n   - §4.4: Immune — linear projection (π0 + X-VLA, controlled comparison with OpenVLA)\n   - §4.5: VTR overcomes cliff-edge (M-curve flat + architecture independence)\n5. **Analysis**:\n   - §5.1: Why pixel_shuffle creates entanglement (spatial folding + controlled comparison)\n   - §5.2: Diagnostic metric — Entropy + CV composite (D104, 4-model validation)\n   - §5.3: Re-encoding mechanism (M=128 passthrough + MLP adapter + M-curve flat D105)\n6. **Discussion**: Design implications, limitations (LIBERO only, no real robot), future work\n7. **Conclusion**: 5 claims summary\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### RC1: Only 2 models → **FULLY ADDRESSED**\n**Response**: Now 5 models (SmolVLA, OpenVLA, OpenVLA-OFT, π0, X-VLA) across 3 connector families (pixel-shuffle, 3-layer MLP, linear projection) and 4 model sizes (450M-7B). Each connector class has ≥2 models.\n\n### RC2: Model size confound (450M vs 7B) → **FULLY ADDRESSED**\n**Response**: π0 (3.3B, linear) is pruning-immune while OpenVLA (7B, MLP) degrades 61% at k=0.1. Larger model is MORE vulnerable, not less — directly falsifies \"larger models are more robust\" hypothesis. X-VLA (880M, linear) also immune, matching π0 behavior despite 4× smaller. Size is not the explanatory variable; connector type is.\n\n### RC3: Why not test SmolVLA without pixel_shuffle?\n**Response**: SmolVLA's connector is integral (LLM expects 128 tokens, not 1024). Removing it requires retraining from scratch. OpenVLA comparison isolates the connector's role.\n\n### RC4: Smart compression — is re-encoding or compression the mechanism?\n**Response**: Two independent lines of evidence. (1) M=128 passthrough control: M=128 (no compression) = 59.8% vs M=32 (75% compression) = 61.6%. The +15pp over baseline comes from re-encoding; compression is a free bonus. (2) Architecture control: MLP adapter (65.2%) ≈ cross-attention (61.6%) with CI overlap — re-encoding works regardless of the specific mechanism. Both lines converge: the causal factor is learned re-encoding, not cross-attention specifically nor compression.\n\n### RC5: Relationship to VLA-IAP\n**Response**: VLA-IAP's 97.8% on OpenVLA is consistent with our finding: MLP projector preserves token independence. VLA-IAP untested on pixel_shuffle VLAs; our geometric prior experiments (0%) predict it would fail.\n\n### RC6: Is this just \"different models behave differently\"?\n**Response**: We identify the specific mechanism (pixel_shuffle spatial folding) and provide evidence for WHY. Concrete design implications: future VLAs wanting efficiency should avoid pixel_shuffle or include learned re-encoding modules.\n\n## All Experimental Evidence (confirmed)\n\n### SmolVLA Cliff-Edge\n- Fine-tuned baseline (Object): 44.8% (224/500)\n- 6 pruning conditions (random/ACIS/ToMe × k=0.5/0.7/0.9): all 0%\n- Geometric prior (Sobel) k=0.5/0.7/0.9: all 0%\n- Attention entropy: 0.952 (flat, TV=0.05)\n- Official checkpoint Object: 59.2% → 0% at k=0.5\n- Official checkpoint Spatial: 43.8% → 0.6% (3/500, CI [0.2%, 1.7%]) at k=0.5 — near-zero, confirms cliff-edge across suites\n\n### OpenVLA Degradation Curve\n- k=1.0: 74.0% (370/500)\n- k=0.95: 69.4% (-6% relative)\n- k=0.9: 62.0% (-16% relative)\n- k=0.7: 49.0% (245/500, -34% relative)\n- k=0.5: 37.4% (187/500, -49% relative)\n- k=0.3: 20.6% (103/500, -72% relative)\n- Entropy: 0.639 (progressive sharpening, TV=0.50)\n\n### Cross-Model Pruning Taxonomy (NEW — D100 confirmed all L2_norm_zero_out)\n| Model | Connector | Tokens | k=0.1 norm_L1 | k=0.5 norm_L1 | Class |\n|-------|-----------|--------|---------------|---------------|-------|\n| π0 (3.3B) | Pure linear proj | 256 | 0.976 (-2.4%) | 0.983 | Immune |\n| X-VLA (880M) | Linear + BART 12L | 50 | ~1.00 | ~1.00 | Threshold-graded (k=0.7: 56.2%) |\n| OpenVLA (7B) | 3-layer MLP | 256 | 1.608 (+61%) | 1.167 (+17%) | Smooth graded |\n| OFT (7B) | 3-layer MLP | 256 | pending (M031) | — | (entropy=0.667≈Std) |\n| SmolVLA (450M) | Pixel-shuffle | 128 | — | 0% cliff | Catastrophic |\n\nDiagnostic metrics (D104+D106: CV as continuous predictor):\n| Model | Connector (effective) | Entropy | CV | Pruning Behavior |\n|-------|----------------------|---------|------|------------------|\n| π0 | Pure linear proj | 0.959 | 0.038 | Immune (offline, k=0.1 improves) |\n| X-VLA | Linear proj + BART enc (12L) | **0.614** | **0.151** | Threshold-graded: robust k≥0.9, cliff k=0.7 (56.2%) |\n| OpenVLA | 3-layer MLP (GELU) | 0.661 | 0.215 | Graded (74→37.4% at k=0.5) |\n| OFT | 3-layer MLP (GELU) | 0.667 | — | (entropy≈Standard, confirms connector) |\n| SmolVLA | Pixel-shuffle (16×) | 0.952 | **0.314** | Catastrophic (0% at k=0.95) |\n\nKey insights (updated with X-VLA data):\n1. **CV is a continuous predictor**: 0.038 → 0.151 → 0.215 → 0.314 maps to decreasing pruning tolerance. NOT a 3-class discrete classifier.\n2. **X-VLA CV=0.151 correctly predicted its intermediate behavior** — closer to OpenVLA (0.215) than π0 (0.038), matching k=0.7 SR ≈ OpenVLA k=0.7.\n3. **X-VLA entropy=0.614 ≈ OpenVLA 0.661** — both have BART/MLP non-linear mixing. X-VLA is NOT \"high entropy\" like π0.\n4. **Effective connector complexity matters more than nominal label**: X-VLA is \"linear proj\" but BART encoder introduces 12 transformer layers of mixing. Paper should discuss \"effective connector\" = projection + any intermediate processing.\n5. OFT entropy 0.667 ≈ Standard 0.661 → action head does not affect attention structure\n6. Framing: CV captures \"degree of non-linear mixing in the vision-to-LLM pathway.\" More mixing → higher CV → less tolerant of pruning.\n\n### Smart Compress (Learned Re-encoding)\nCross-attention (VTC) M sweep (3-seed where available):\n- M=16: **56.9% ± 1.3pp** (3-seed: 55.8/58.4/56.5) — 87.5% compression, ZERO loss vs M=128\n- M=32: 60.1±1.3% (3-seed: 61.6/59.2/59.6)\n- M=64: 62.0±2.3% (3-seed: 61.6/60.0/64.4)\n- M=96: 54.5±3.2% (3-seed: 52.6/58.2/52.8) — highest variance\n- M=128: 58.0±3.5pp (3-seed: 59.8/54.0/60.2) — passthrough control\n- **D102 CONFIRMED: M-curve is flat.** M=16 (56.9%) = M=128 (58.0%), difference < 1.1pp. VTC achieves near-lossless compression at ANY ratio for pixel-shuffle connector. All M values within ±5pp of baseline ~59%.\n\nMLP adapter (M=32, 3-seed):\n- seed42: 65.2% (326/500), seed43: 64.6% (323/500), seed44: 60.4% (302/500)\n- Mean: 63.4% ± 2.6 vs VTC M=32 mean 60.1% ± 1.3\n- t-test p≈0.15, no significant difference — confirms architecture-independence\n\n### D109: VTR on OpenVLA (MLP projector) — FAILED\n- C4-ext M=128 at 20K steps: **0% SR** (0/44 episodes before eval termination)\n- Failure mode: model outputs identical action every timestep (severe overfitting)\n- Interpretation: VTR adds unnecessary complexity for MLP projector tokens (already individually meaningful). VTR specifically solves pixel-shuffle entanglement.\n- VTC-only M=32 and M=64 training in progress on westd-41074:2-3\n\n### Cross-Suite VTR Generalization (SmolVLA)\n- M=32 Spatial: 53.2% (vs official Spatial baseline 43.8%, vs pruning 0.6% — recovery from cliff-edge AND exceeds uncompressed baseline)\n- M=32 Goal: 56.4% (vs official Goal baseline 63.0%, vs pruning 0% — recovery from cliff-edge but below uncompressed baseline; frame as compression-recovery, not performance boost)\n- Note: VTR trained on Object suite, evaluated cross-suite. Official baselines from different checkpoint (official vs LoRA-FT); comparisons should emphasize recovery from 0% rather than absolute performance vs uncompressed.\n\n### Pairwise Statistical Tests (Welch's t-test, n=3 per condition)\n- M=32 VTC vs M=32 MLP: p=0.150 (not significant) — confirms architecture-independence\n- M=64 VTC vs M=96 VTC: p=0.034 (significant) — M=96 dip confirmed real\n- M=32 MLP vs M=96 VTC: p=0.022 (significant) — M=96 underperforms\n- M=32/M=64/M=128 VTC cluster: all pairwise p>0.25 — robust across M values\n- Bonferroni note: no pair survives correction at 10 tests; n=3 limits power (~0.2 for medium effects)\n\n### Supplementary\n- Inference latency: 6% speedup at M=32\n- KV-cache: 75% memory reduction at M=32\n","numLines":267,"startLine":1,"totalLines":267}}}
