{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FJgmUksNfA5iokzSHyuqoh","type":"tool_result","content":"30\t%% ABSTRACT\n31\t%% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models process hundreds of visual tokens per frame, creating a bottleneck for real-time robotic deployment. While inference-time token reduction methods have shown promise, their effectiveness varies dramatically across architectures for reasons that remain unexplained. We present the first systematic study of token pruning robustness across VLA architectures, testing 6 models spanning 4 connector families and model sizes from 450M to 7B parameters. We discover that the \\emph{effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline}---not model size---determines pruning tolerance along a continuous spectrum. Pure linear projection (\\pi_0) is pruning-immune (loss \\emph{improves} 2.4\\% at 90\\% token removal). Linear projection followed by deep post-processing (X-VLA, 12-layer BART encoder) exhibits a threshold-graded pattern: robust at $k \\geq 0.9$ (97.6\\% SR) but cliffs at $k=0.7$ (56.2\\%). Learnable cross-attention queries (RoboFlamingo, Perceiver Resampler) show gentle graded degradation. Multi-layer MLP connectors (OpenVLA) degrade smoothly ($k=0.5 \\to 37.4\\%$ SR). Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA) collapse catastrophically---removing just 5\\% of tokens yields 0\\% success rate. The coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance scores predicts this spectrum: $\\text{CV} = 0.038$ (immune) $\\to 0.11$ (gentle) $\\to 0.151$ (threshold) $\\to 0.215$ (smooth) $\\to 0.314$ (catastrophic). Based on this analysis, we propose a Diagnose-then-Compress framework: (1) a zero-cost CV diagnostic that predicts pruning tolerance from a single forward pass, and (2) Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module ($\\sim$22M parameters) that provides universal token compression across connector types---recovering pixel-shuffle from 0\\% to 60.1\\% SR at 75\\% compression, and achieving 45--58\\% SR on MLP projectors at 75--87.5\\% compression where hard pruning retains only 8--21\\%.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t%% ============================================================\n37\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n38\t%% ============================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6ee454c3-2f7e-4079-9b70-bcb44e9beab1","uuid":"42686653-b276-4a58-95d2-e97f91455759","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:27:39.065Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T155932/best/paper.tex","content":"%% ABSTRACT\n%% ============================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nVision-Language-Action (VLA) models process hundreds of visual tokens per frame, creating a bottleneck for real-time robotic deployment. While inference-time token reduction methods have shown promise, their effectiveness varies dramatically across architectures for reasons that remain unexplained. We present the first systematic study of token pruning robustness across VLA architectures, testing 6 models spanning 4 connector families and model sizes from 450M to 7B parameters. We discover that the \\emph{effective vision-to-LLM projection pipeline}---not model size---determines pruning tolerance along a continuous spectrum. Pure linear projection (\\pi_0) is pruning-immune (loss \\emph{improves} 2.4\\% at 90\\% token removal). Linear projection followed by deep post-processing (X-VLA, 12-layer BART encoder) exhibits a threshold-graded pattern: robust at $k \\geq 0.9$ (97.6\\% SR) but cliffs at $k=0.7$ (56.2\\%). Learnable cross-attention queries (RoboFlamingo, Perceiver Resampler) show gentle graded degradation. Multi-layer MLP connectors (OpenVLA) degrade smoothly ($k=0.5 \\to 37.4\\%$ SR). Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA) collapse catastrophically---removing just 5\\% of tokens yields 0\\% success rate. The coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance scores predicts this spectrum: $\\text{CV} = 0.038$ (immune) $\\to 0.11$ (gentle) $\\to 0.151$ (threshold) $\\to 0.215$ (smooth) $\\to 0.314$ (catastrophic). Based on this analysis, we propose a Diagnose-then-Compress framework: (1) a zero-cost CV diagnostic that predicts pruning tolerance from a single forward pass, and (2) Vision Token Re-encoding (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module ($\\sim$22M parameters) that provides universal token compression across connector types---recovering pixel-shuffle from 0\\% to 60.1\\% SR at 75\\% compression, and achieving 45--58\\% SR on MLP projectors at 75--87.5\\% compression where hard pruning retains only 8--21\\%.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%% ============================================================\n%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n%% ============================================================\n\\section{Introduction}","numLines":10,"startLine":30,"totalLines":487}}}
